I don't know what you mean by "the present p".

The key point I wanted to make is that "p" is simply a proposition/state-of-
affairs, not an assertion (tho it may be griceanly understood as an assertion).
AFAICS, expressing an emotion of happiness about a proposition/soa does
not entail that the p/soa is the case, and the belief that it does entail
that the p/soa is the case is a malglico carryover from "to be happy that p"
(& cf. "to be happy for X to do Y", which doesn't entail X has done Y).

